%description:

Tcp.timestampSupport:changed -- a change during the run applies to the next connection.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "Each connection reads it at its open, so a run can change it as the
host-wide sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps".

The classes: on (true, the default), off (false; LegacyTcp), changed (a change during the run,
because the parameter is @mutable: each connection reads it at its open). This test separates "changed" from "off": with false from the start, the
first SYN has no Timestamps option.

Host under test: host1, which opens a connection at 0.1 s and another at 1.5 s; an action
turns the parameter off at 1 s. Observation: level 1, the first SYN of host1 has a Timestamps
option, and the second has none.

%# parameter: Tcp.timestampSupport
%# class: changed
%# value: true
%# key: *.host1.tcp.timestampSupport
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpTimestampSupportChanged")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertEvent([](const MatchContext& context) { return tcpOptionCount(context.event.packet, inet::tcp::TCPOPTION_TIMESTAMP) == 1; })
                  .describe("the first SYN of host1, with a Timestamps option")
                  .within(0.5))
        .act(action().at(1.0).setParameter("host1.tcp", "timestampSupport", "false"))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertEvent([](const MatchContext& context) { return tcpOptionCount(context.event.packet, inet::tcp::TCPOPTION_TIMESTAMP) == 0; })
                  .describe("the second SYN of host1, without a Timestamps option")
                  .within(1.0));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpTimestampSupportChanged compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.timestampSupport = true    # the parameter under test
*.host1.numApps = 2
*.host1.app[1].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[1].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[1].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[1].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[1].sendBytes = 100B
*.host1.app[1].tClose = 1000s
*.host1.app[1].tOpen = 1.5s
*.host1.app[1].tSend = 1.6s
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpTimestampSupportChanged: PASS
